First, Noah checked the parts one at a time. A a faded blue tag gave a useful clue. The part that looked most important was not actually broken; it had shifted slightly. Noah asked a nearby adult for permission to test a careful adjustment. After 11 minutes of work, the object was working again.

The small success changed the plan for the rest of the afternoon. Instead of putting the object away, Noah showed two friends what had happened. Together they wrote 4 short steps on a card so that the same problem would be easier to solve next time.

By the end of the day, the group had not created a complicated solution. They had simply paid attention, tested one idea at a time and recorded what worked. Noah decided that this was the most useful part of the experience: a new way to notice landmarks.',NULL,NULL,NULL,3,'READING','PUBLISHED',NULL);

